\begin_layout Title
Social Capital Investments and Inequality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Status motivations have generated an immense interest at a time when social mobility has been relatively stagnant - but the consequences of inequality on labour participation are still poorly understood.
 To explain status motivations under environments of income inequality,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
we treat opportunities for mobility 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
an investment towards future economic success.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
develops a theoretical framework to examine how income inequality shapes status competition through differential access to social capital
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The effect of relative incomes on the agents’ consumption and labour decisions is then explored in an economy with a dynamic hierarchy of rich and poor positions that participants are able to move between.
 With diminishing returns to wealth utility optimised by the participants,
 the equilibrium conditions indicate that while a rise in income inequality provides more incentive to participate in status competitions,
 such competitions are infeasible when inequality rises too much.
 As participants bear costs for the mobility,
 the equilibrium conditions suggest only two states in the long-run - one where both rich and poor strive for status and the other where only the rich participate in status competitions.
 IMPLICATIONS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
results offer a novel explanation for the persistence of status anxiety in unequal societies and clarify how subjective motivations can endure even when objective mobility becomes unattainable.
 The findings underscore the importance of distinguishing cultural from structural dimensions of status in understanding the economic effects of inequality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Rising inequality doesn’t just affect incomes-it reshapes how people pursue status.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
s paper models status as something earned through social capital (like education or networks),
 but shows that inequality limits access to such investments.
 While people still care about their status,
 fewer can realistically improve it.
 As a result,
 the culture of status-seeking continues even as actual mobility declines.
 This helps explain growing frustration and anxiety in unequal societies,
