% Inequality effects on labour are uncertain - whether it motivates participation or not is debated.
 At a time when we are supposed to be rushing towards luxury goods because of high inequality (Frank),
 it has also been argued that the rush came about in the post-war period that was far less unequal than before (Hirsch).
 The paper identifies two primary issues with current approeaches to status and motivation in economics and focuses on a particular status-formation mechanism to explain how inequality affects motivation to participate in the economy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% First,
 it distinguishes subjective status-the individual's internalized sense of social position- from objective status,
 the status positions with real consequences for economic opportunity.
 While the consumer focus on subjective status may have increased in recent times,
 status differences caused by long-term inequalities may continue to set limits on labour or consumption towards objective status.
 The higher focus on status in our times of rising inequality underscores the subjective sense of status - while the permanent status differences that set limits on efforts towards status circuscribe the objective sense of status.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% The second issue addressed in the paper is the use of static attributes such as race,
 gender or regional identity to define status.
 We argue that the approach hinders our understanding of how inequality affects status-related motivations.
 Since status itself is subject to change in response to growth and inequality,
 the delineation of effects on inequality on status motivations requires us to endogenise status in an economic model.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% The paper thus focuses on a dynamic view of objective status where it is acquired through social capital investments (see Weber,
 Goode and lit.
 on durability and closure).
 The paper then investigates how social capital expenditures - which circumscribe the motivations pertaining to objective status - may be limited by inequality due to income constraints imposed by objective status differences.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% The long-term equilibrium outline three important results on the effects on inequality.
 First,
 the equilbrium conditions show that although inequality does encourage social capital investments,
 the higher levels of inequality cannot sustain a game of status driven by social capital investments with diminishing returns to wealth.
 The observations made by both Frank and Hirsch (i.e.
 whether inequality increases or decreases obsession with status goods) are thus both true in their own different ways - as constraints to social capital investments as well as participation costs are imposed by inequality while culture - corresponding to subjective status - lets consumers fulfil local status-related pressures.
 Second,
 we find that high participation costs or closure have a key and yet understated role in maintaining status competitions.
 The role of social closure in providing motivation is something that needs to be better underscored in discussions of status consumption.
 Third,
 only two long-term states are possible for status-games of this nature - one in which both poor and rich participate in status competitions and one where only the rich take part in it.
 So long as there exists income stratification,
 the argument that status competitions can favour the poor is made impossible due to declining marginal contribution of social capital investments to wealth utility.
 If investments in social capital do matter,
 then there is no state where only poor aspire to be rich while rich continue to enjoy their high-status.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% Conclusions.
 In the normative sense,
 therefore,
 the argument from Hirsch holds (everyone stands on tiptoes no-one sees better) - that the limited number of rich does limit mobility and renders many aspirational pursuits rather futile.
 That a subjective motivation may continue to thrive with inequality while the motivation in an objective sense dwindles,
 is also aligned with research on perceptions of meritocracy (Mijs).
 Status goals may appear as a need - resulting in a status anxiety that arise when such needs aren't fulfilled.
 But eventually the game of mobility cannot appeal to the poor for long in the objective sense,
 and the seeming dissonances in economic behaviour becomes likely.
 Our model provides a way to better explain how notions of social mobility and participation (closure) are shaped by inequality (income differences) and influence motivation and consumption (connect with the opening statement).
\end_layout

