 A distinction between objective and subjective aspects of status helps us better delineate how inequality may influence particular mechanisms of status formation.
 The apparent contradiction between conventional and modern accounts of status consumption is also resolved if one draws a distinction between subjective and objective aspects of status.
 More specifically,
 while the conventional view argues that a higher demand for positional goods had been a result of relative income equality in the post-war period 
