\begin_layout Standard
The second issue that we highlight in the current paper is the need to endogenise status within formal economic models for studying effects of inequality.
 Despite a growing literature that has explored status formation through linguistic communication and competitions,
 status has more often been understood in economics as a set of static qualitative attribues.
 The conventional thinking
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{HurstSocialIneq2016}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 treats status qualitatively - operationalising low status via categorical group membership-race,
 gender,
 ethnicity,
 or region - an approach that imposes limitations on quantitative assessments of status and its improvements.
 More particularly,
 since status perceptions also change in response to economic growth and inequality,
 a failure to endogenise status in an economic model makes it difficult to understand the effects of inequality on status motivations.
\end_layout

