\begin_layout Standard
The presented approach attempts to address these issues by focusing on a particular mechanism of how inequality influences participation.
 It considers objective aspects of status that is influenced by social capital investments.
 In contrast to status ascribed by static social groupings,
 it is viewed as a dynamic state that is changed through expenditures aimed at maintaining one's position within privileged networks.
 The long-term equilibrium conditions reveal three observation on how inequality shapes status motivations.
 First,
 it becomes clear that while inequality can initially encourage social capital investment,
 high levels of inequality impose constraints that make status competition unsustainable - reconciling Frank’s and Hirsch’s seemingly opposing views Cultural adaptation may encourage individuals to meet local status pressures (subjective status),
 even though the structural mobility (objective status) continues to decline.
 Second,
 high participation costs,
 driven by social closure,
 are crucial in maintaining status hierarchies and may serve as necessity for status competitions.
 Third,
 the results permit only two stable equilibria:
 one where both rich and poor compete for status,
 and another where only the rich participate.
\end_layout

