\begin_layout Standard
A closed-form solution with a diminishing utility from wealth and a threshold level expenditure required for participants to spend (in order to avail social capital) suggests that there are only statues in the long-term equilibrium.
 First,
 where the rich as well as the poor participant participates in the status competitions.
 Second,
 where only the rich participant engages in status competitions.
 In other words,
 the state where only the poor participate in status consumption is ruled out in the above formulation.
 Thus if the social capital investments were to be seen as an exchange between rich and the poor 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{GalbraithAffluent,HirschSocialLimits}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 the competitions where poor strive for status must ultimately become infeasible (except when relying thinly on a reference-dependent utility) and lead to only poor withdrawing from status competitions.
 In other words,
 while the poor may be incentivised for status,
 the long-term equilibrium is one where both poor and rich must strive for status - a result that seems aligned with the observations on positional consumption in the post-war era 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{HirschSocialLimits,FrankPond}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The model also details how participation costs - along with higher income differences - may have a key role to play in the rise or decline in social capital investments (e.g.
 education or network-building activities).
 More particularly,
 social capital investments are likely to be subject to a discouragement through high participation costs while still being incentivised in an environment of higher income differences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The conclusions are also relevant to the status consumption literature - which often views expenditures on status goods as wasteful or as means to signal high status.
 The paper argues instead that many status-related expenditures-such as higher education,
 neighborhood choice,
 or professional affiliation-may serve roles in enabling access to upward mobility - emphasising that social capital is not a mere symbol of status but a mechanism for acquiring it.
 The social capital perspective clarifies a critical ambiguity in the literature:
 whether rising inequality leads to an increase or decrease in status-seeking behavior.
 On the one hand,
 inequality imposes income constraints that limit participation in costly status competitions.
 On the other hand,
 the cultural emphasis on meritocracy and status achievement often intensifies in unequal societies,
 leading individuals to continue striving despite structural obstacles.
 The model presented in the paper helps explain why status anxiety can persist even in the face of stagnant mobility,
 especially in developed economies.
 A separation of subjective and objective status is key to understatnding these mechanisms.
 While subjective status captures internal perceptions and psychological responses,
 objective status relates to an individual’s actual structural position and their capacity to translate status investments into material returns.
 
\end_layout

