\begin_layout Standard
The relationship between inequality,
 motivation,
 and status-seeking behavior has generated extensive debate across both economics and sociology.
 While early economic thought often emphasized the functional role of inequality as a motivational force—
providing incentives for effort,
 investment,
 and innovation—
more recent perspectives have challenged this view,
 particularly in the context of constrained social mobility and stratified opportunity structures.
 One could defend inequality on grounds of efficiency and incentive compatibility 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{BeckerTomesEquilibMobility1979}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 - since differential rewards signal effort or productivity and sustain motivation.
 However,
 competing empirical and behavioral studies suggest that inequality may,
 beyond a certain threshold,
 undermine labor force participation,
 trust,
 and long-term productivity 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{PerottiGrowthIncomeDis1993,AlesinaPerotti1996,StiglitzPriceofIneq2012}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 These contrasting perspectives remain visible in recent debates,
 reflecting an unresolved tension:
 whether inequality spurs economic participation or discourages it by limiting realistic pathways to advancement 
