\begin_layout Plain Layout

%[Weberian view and deference view - we take the weberian view]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the notion of status as a set of fixed attributes,
 the common empirical view for studies in economics as well as sociology has been based on hierarchy and social deference 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ColemanSocialTheory1990,goldthorpesociology2000"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This is the view inherent in occupational status surveys widely used in studies (e.g.,
 the EGP classes or the Goldthorpe class scheme).
 The other important view of status is based on Weberian conflict (see 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{parsonsInfluencesa1963,blauSocialStructure1974}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 - where no rational order of status is stated as a justification for hierarchy.
 In either of the approaches,
 a fundamental issue that has been overlooked is the reconciliation of subjective and objective aspects of status 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{wegener1992prestigeconcepts}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 .
 To recall,
 objective status is the observed reality of status differences while subjective status circumscribes the effect of status differences on individual behaviour.
 As 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{wegener1992prestigeconcepts}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 elaborates,
 the status measured in occupational surveys can be at odds with the self-reported perceptions of status.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%[Explicity link relying on Goode]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Establishing an explicit link between social and economic goals of a participant helps to address the issue of reconciliation between subjective and objective aspects of status.
 More particularly,
 the social capital link used in the current paper allows us to defined the scope of effects of income inequality on consumer motivations to participate.
 The competition-based view of status used in the paper - drawing from 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{goode1978celebrationheroes}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 - emphasises how institutions discourage or promote the success-determining attributes in a society.
 Goode's framework also rejects the view of status as a tradable-power 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{ColemanSocialTheory1990}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 by challenging the notion of there being a fixed total amount of status in society.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%[Utility function and instrumental view]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While status is linked with economic outcomes in the model,
 status is not viewed as a wealth-determined goal.
 Instead,
 the paper follows 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{postlewaite1998socialbasisinterdeppreferences}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in considering status only of an indirect role in maximisation of wealth utility.
 Thus the rank of the participant (rich or poor status) does not directly contribute to utility and but matters only through a higher income that contributes to maximisation of the participant's expected wealth utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%[objective and subjective aspects are both important - we emphasis the objective side - subjective studies should emphasis their subjective side rather than objective]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The approach also offers a renewed perspective on visibility-based models in the status consumption literature.
 When status is treated as fixed and not directly measurable,
 empirical approaches often reduce status consumption to a form of futile expenditure.
 Such a framing,
 however,
 may be at adds with more functionalist perspectives (e.g.
 from 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{webereconomysociety1925,goode1978celebrationheroes}
\end_layout

\end_inset

) where status-seeking serves social purposes.
 For status goods that simultaneously enhance social capital,
 the effects of inequality may diverge from the predictions of visibility-driven models.
 Misinterpretations of status pressures are also possible if one fails to highlight that status consumption may reflect only the subjective perceptions of status.
 Likewise,
 overreaching conclusions about status-seeking behaviour (esp of the poor) may be drawn if one ignores the subjective aspects of status.
 The implications of this paper are situated primarily within the domain of objective status—
where status motivations are observable through investments in social capital that yield tangible economic returns.
 Nonetheless,
 more detailed views of subjective of status provide a crucial dimension to research in economic participation.
 For example,
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{MijsInequalityMobility2019}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 focus on subjective data to detail how a rise in inequality might have cemented a belief in meritocracy in the developed economies and coincided with segregation rather than a healthy competition.
 When examining status—
whether from a normative or descriptive perspective—
it is essential that the conclusions regarding its implications reconcile both its subjective and objective aspects.
 
\end_layout

