\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Probability-of-winning"

\end_inset

Probability of winning 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}(\text{win})$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}(\text{win})$
\end_inset

 for poor and rich participants with varying 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As is clear from Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Probability-of-winning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and above results (see summaries in Tables 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Results_summary_poor"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Results_summary_rich"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 the condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu<1/2$
\end_inset

 - indicating that curated skills are given more importance that raw-talent in the competitions - means that the poor participant is strictly better off (than facing probability 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

) if she participates while the rich participant doesn't participate (Case II).
 Also,
 the poor participant is better off participating for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu<\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 when the rich participant does participate as it raises the probability to 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

.
 That the competition becomes uncompetitive both when threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 falls too much (letting both participants participate) or rises too much (letting both participants withdraw) also follows from the properties of the winning probability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that the rich participant could withdraw and face either 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

 or lower probability (when the poor participant participates).
 By participating,
 therefore,
 the rich participant would either face probability 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

 (when poor participant also participates) or better (if the poor participant withdraws).
 Considering the probabilities alone,
 the rich participant would always participate.
 If the rich participant always participates,
 then so long as budget constraints permit,
 the poor participant is also better off participating since not participating brings her probability to win below 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

.
 If the utility was from probability alone,
 then both participants would participate and face 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

 probability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 it's not the probability to win that the participants optimise (maximise).
 A more accurate representation of the real-world is a game where participants face a finite life-time and utility from accumulated assets instead.
 Thus,
 what follows is the case where the participants optimise an intertemporally additive utility over their life-time.
 The life-time in the model is represented with two time-horizons - where the second-time horizon is one before the end of life when all wealth accumulated by either participant must disappear.
 With no accumulations at the end of life,
 both rich and poor participants must decide to spend all their incomes 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 (for poor and rich participants respectively so that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}<y_{2}$
\end_inset

) towards the promotion game.
 In the first-stage,
 however,
 the participants decide the amount to be spent towards promotion based upon the utility from wealth they obtain across the two time-horizons that span their lifetime.
 For the poor participant,
 the total utility to be optimised (assuming EUT) under the intertemporally additive utility 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

,
 a winning probability function 
\begin_inset Formula $W(x)$
\end_inset

 (for expenditure 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

) and a discount-factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 is 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
u(y_{1}-\nu_{1})+\frac{1}{\delta}((1-W(y_{1}))u(y_{1})+W(y_{1})u(y_{2}))
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 for the rich participant,
 the total utility to be optimised (assuming EUT) is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
u(y_{2}-\nu_{2})+\frac{1}{\delta}((1-W(y_{2}))u(y_{1})+W(y_{2})u(y_{2}))
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

