\begin_layout Standard
With above utilities,
 the conditions for whether the Case I,
 II,
 III,
 IV can become Nash equilibria (see Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:prisoners_dilemma_cases"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) are now discussed.
 A discussion of the implications for the effect of income differences on expenditure towards status then follows.
 It is worth remarking that the discussion of the effect of the threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 and the income differences 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}-y_{1}$
\end_inset

 on participation (expenditure) assumes that the exogenous parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 is stable with respect to short-term changes in threshold investment 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 as well as disposable incomes 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1},y_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For ease of notation we define 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1},p_{2},p_{1}'$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}'$
\end_inset

 - which correspond to the
\series bold
 two-player 
\series default
model discussed above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
p_{1}\equiv\frac{\mu}{6(1-\mu)}\nonumber \\
p_{2}\equiv1-p_{1}\nonumber \\
p_{1}'\equiv\frac{6-7\mu}{6(1-\mu)}\label{eq:probabilities_2player}\\
p_{2}'\equiv1-p_{1}'\nonumber 
\end{gather}

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The choices for the poor and rich participants using a general intertemporally additive function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(A)$
\end_inset

 (for assets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

) are shown in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:prisoners_dilemma"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 As we can verify from Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Probability-of-winning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 that we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}<1/2<p_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}'>1/2>p_{2}'$
\end_inset

 for all values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu<1/2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\text{Given }\mu<1/2,\text{we have}\label{eq:probability_inequality}\\
p_{1}<1/2<p_{2}\nonumber \\
p_{1}'>1/2>p_{2}'\nonumber 
\end{gather}

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice the Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:prisoners_dilemma"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 represents the condition when the budget is non-binding i.e.
 the poor participant is able to spend 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

.
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}<y_{1}<y_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 When the poor participant faces a binding constraint i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}<\overline{\nu}<y_{2}$
\end_inset

 ,
 then the rich participant knows that the best case probability to win for the poor participant is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The rich participant's decision to participate would depend only on utility from savings and the relative advantage from gain in probability.
 This is elaborated further in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Binding-constraint"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the number of poor participants is likely to be higher than that of rich participants in a typical competition,
 the unequal number of poor and rich participants seems also worth considering in the model.
 Setting 
\begin_inset Formula $N=3,P=2$
\end_inset

 in the equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:CaseI_general"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:CaseII_general"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:CaseIV_general"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 for example,
 one can interpret the 
\series bold
three-player
\series default
 model with the following values of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1},p_{2},p_{1}'$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}'$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
p_{1}=\frac{\mu}{8(1-\mu)}\nonumber \\
p_{2}=1-2p_{1}\nonumber \\
p_{1}'=\frac{19\mu^{2}-40\mu+20}{40(1-\mu)^{2}}\label{eq:probabilities_3player}\\
p_{2}'=1-2p_{1}'\nonumber 
\end{gather}

\end_inset


\end_layout

