\begin_layout Subsubsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Binding-constraint"

\end_inset

Binding constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
noindent
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming EUT participants,
 consider the simpler case of binding constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}<\overline{\nu}<y_{2}$
\end_inset

 first .
 With the assumption of a stability in long-term participant behaviour (which itself is a result of observed probability of success),
 mixed-strategies are not considered relevant in the current set up.
 Considering pure strategies for participation,
 the rich participant would thus participate only when the utility from participating would be higher than from not participating i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{p_{2}u(y_{2})+(1-p_{2})u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{2})+\frac{u(y_{2})+u(y_{1})}{2\delta}$
\end_inset

 .
 This results in the following condition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{(p_{2}-1/2)(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta} & >u(y_{2})-u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With income differences being the same an increase in participation cost 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 discourages participation since RHS increases with 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\because u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})$
\end_inset

 is decreasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

) and may fail the above condition.
 Similarly,
 with participation cost 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 being the same,
 an increase in income-difference 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}-y_{1}$
\end_inset

 is in the rich participant's interest whereas a narrowing of income differences reduces incentive for the participant to participate by possibly failing the above condition.
 All else being the same,
 higher impatience (lower time-discounted value of money or a higher 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

) would also discourage participation.
 Therefore,
 when budget is binding for the poor participant i.e.
 if the poor participant cannot participate due to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}<\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

,
 then narrower income differences,
 higher participation costs and higher patience for savings (common for both rich and poor participants) all encourage the rich participant to withdraw from the game.
 This also means that wider income differences (
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}-y_{1}$
\end_inset

) combined with lower participation costs (
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

) may together help maintain the equilibrium where only the rich participates.
 Similarly,
 wider income differences ( higher 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}-y_{1}$
\end_inset

 ) and more patience for savings (higher 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

) would together maintain the incentive for the rich participant to participate.
 These conclusions very much fit with the sociological observations made in the literature about status 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "HirschSocialLimits,GalbraithEssential"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Non-Binding-constraint"

\end_inset

Non-Binding constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
noindent
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When budget is non-binding,
 then participation of the rich participant is her dominant strategy if the condition is also 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{2})+\frac{u(y_{2})p_{2}'+(1-p_{2}')u(y_{1})}{\delta}<u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{u(y_{2})+u(y_{1})}{2\delta})$
\end_inset

 i.e.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{(1/2-p_{2}')(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta} & >u(y_{2})-u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The inferences on how 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}-y_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 influence the participation (see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Binding-constraint"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) remain the same for the above condition.
 To discuss the more general Nash equilibria,
 consider the conditions for all of the Cases I,
 II,
 III and IV to become a Nash equilibrium.
 For ease of notation we define the following for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu},y_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
U_{1} & \equiv u(y_{1})-u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
U_{2} & \equiv u(y_{2})-u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One could view 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{2}$
\end_inset

 as the immediate gain in wealth utility from withdrawing.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 represents a diminishing utility,
 then it is also necessary for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}<y_{2}$
\end_inset

 that 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
U_{2} & <U_{1}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider now that conditions for Case I to be a Nash Equilibrium.
 If Case I were to be a Nash equilibrium,
 then for the rich participant's decision to participate,
 the poor participant must get more utility from withdrawing than from participating and for poor participant's decision to withdraw,
 the rich must get more utility from participating than from withdrawing.
 The following conditions 
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{1})+\frac{p_{1}u(y_{2})+(1-p_{1})u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{\frac{1}{N}u(y_{2})+\frac{1}{N}u(y_{1})}{\delta}\Rightarrow u(y_{1})-u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})>\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{1})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{p_{2}u(y_{2})+(1-p_{2})u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{2})+\frac{\frac{1}{N}u(y_{2})+\frac{1}{N}u(y_{1})}{\delta}\Rightarrow u(y_{2})-u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})<\frac{(p_{2}-1/N)(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 must therefore hold simultaneously
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{1})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}<U_{1}\nonumber \\
U_{2}<\frac{(p_{2}-1/N)(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}\label{eq:caseI_inequalities}
\end{gather}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% (
\backslash
frac{(
\backslash
frac{1}{3}-p_{1})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{
\backslash
delta},
\backslash
frac{(p_{2}-1/3)(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{
\backslash
delta})
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}-\frac{1}{N}\ge\frac{1}{N}-p_{1}$
\end_inset

 (strict inequality follows when the number of poor participants 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is higher than that for rich participants 
\begin_inset Formula $N-P$
\end_inset

 e.g.,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $N=3$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P=2$
\end_inset

),
 this is a fairly general condition for the equilibrium.
 An increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{1}$
\end_inset

 due to higher 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 does not change the equilibrium conditions so long as the corresponding rise 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{2}$
\end_inset

 (due to higher 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 ) does not affect the second inequality.
 In other words,
 a higher 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 favours the equilibrium (Case I) so long as there is an incentive for the rich to participate.
 Likewise,
 a decrease in 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 would be favourable for the equilibrium (Case I) so long as the poor participants do not also have an incentive to participate.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similar effects can be seen with income differences 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}-y_{1}$
\end_inset

 (which increase 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})$
\end_inset

 as well as 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{U_{2}}{U_{1}}$
\end_inset

) if we rewrite Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:caseI_inequalities"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 as 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{1})}{\delta}<\frac{U_{1}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}\\
\frac{U_{2}}{(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}<\frac{(p_{2}-1/N)}{\delta}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Limiting our attention to cases when 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}<y_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 higher 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 for fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 decreases 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{U_{2}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}$
\end_inset

and makes it more likely for the rich to participate while it also decreases
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{U_{1}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}$
\end_inset

 by raising the stakes enough for the poor to participate (making it less likely for first inequality to be fulfilled).
 It is easy to see that higher impatience 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 encourages both poor and rich to withdraw.
 As would be evident,
 the equilibrium conditions corresponding to Case II are far more stringent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If Case II is a Nash Equilibrium,
 then for rich's decision to withdraw,
 the poor participant should get more utility from participating than from withdrawing and for poor participant's decision to participate,
 the rich participant should get more utility from withdrawing than from participating.
 This means that the following hold 
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{p_{1}'u(y_{2})+(1-p_{1}')u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{1})+\frac{\frac{1}{N}u(y_{2})+\frac{1}{N}u(y_{1})}{\delta}\Rightarrow u(y_{1})-u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})<\frac{(p_{1}'-1/N)(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{2})+\frac{p_{2}'u(y_{2})+(1-p_{2}')u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{\frac{1}{N}u(y_{2})+\frac{1}{N}u(y_{1})}{\delta}\Rightarrow u(y_{2})-u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})>\frac{(1/N-p_{2}')(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 simultaneously,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
U_{1}<\frac{(p_{1}'-1/N)(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}\\
U_{2}>\frac{(1/N-p_{2}')(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{(p_{1}'-1/N)(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}<\frac{(1/N-p_{2}')(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset

 ,
 the above cannot be satisfied so long as we have a diminishing utility which necessitates 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{2}<U_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus an equilibrium with Case II which incentivises the poor to participate but not the rich is possible only with a reference dependent utility with 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{1}<U_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{1}<U_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 we can write above as 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{U_{1}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}<\frac{(p_{1}'-1/N)}{\delta}<\frac{(1/N-p_{2}')}{\delta}<\frac{U_{2}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that despite a reference-dependent utility,
 a higher 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 or more impatience (
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

) may disincentivise the poor from participation just the same (leading to Case III).
 Similarly,
 higher income differences (an increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 for fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

) could encourage the rich to participate by decreasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{U_{2}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}$
\end_inset

 (leading to Case IV).
 Even with the reference dependent utility,
 the equilibrium with Case II remains a fragile one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If Case III were to be a Nash-Equilibrium,
 then for rich participant's decision to withdraw,
 poor should get more utility from withdrawing than from participating and for poor participant's decision to withdraw,
 the rich participant should get more from withdrawing than from participating.
 The following should thus hold
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{1})+\frac{\frac{1}{N}u(y_{2})+\frac{1}{N}u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{p_{1}'u(y_{2})+(1-p_{1}')u(y_{1})}{\delta}\Rightarrow u(y_{1})-u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})>\frac{(p_{1}'-\frac{1}{N})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{2})+\frac{\frac{1}{N}u(y_{2})+\frac{1}{N}u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{p_{2}u(y_{2})+(1-p_{2})u(y_{1})}{\delta}\Rightarrow u(y_{2})-u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})>\frac{(p_{2}-\frac{1}{N})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
U_{1}>\frac{(p_{1}'-\frac{1}{N})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}\\
U_{2}>\frac{(p_{2}-\frac{1}{N})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}'-\frac{1}{N}\le p_{2}-\frac{1}{N}$
\end_inset

 (strict inequality when number of poor participants is higher),
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{(p_{1}'-\frac{1}{N})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}<\frac{(p_{2}-\frac{1}{N})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{2}<U_{1}$
\end_inset

 .
 A higher 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

 pushes both 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{1}$
\end_inset

 further away from participation but a fall in 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

,
 incentivises the rich participant to participate (leading to Case I).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{U_{1}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}>\frac{(p_{1}'-\frac{1}{N})}{\delta}\\
\frac{U_{2}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}>\frac{(p_{2}-\frac{1}{N})}{\delta}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Higher income differences i.e.
 higher 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 for fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 also decrease 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{U_{2}}{u(y_{2})-u(y_{1})}$
\end_inset

 and incentivise the rich participant to participate (leading to an equilibrium with Case I).
 Conversely,
 if the game is not lucrative for the rich it would automatically be infeasible for the poor i.e.
 if rich withdraws then poor automatically withdraws.
 If there is any net utility from playing the game,
 however,
 Case IV is the likely equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If Case IV were to be a Nash Equilibrium,
 then for rich's decision to participate,
 the poor participant should get more from participating than from withdrawing and for poor's decision to participate,
 the rich participant should get more from participating than from withdrawing.
 Therefore,
 the following
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{\frac{1}{N}u(y_{2})+\frac{1}{N}u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{1})+\frac{p_{1}u(y_{2})+(1-p_{1})u(y_{1})}{\delta}\Rightarrow u(y_{1})-u(y_{1}-\overline{\nu})<\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{1})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})+\frac{\frac{1}{N}u(y_{2})+\frac{1}{N}u(y_{1})}{\delta}>u(y_{2})+\frac{p_{2}'u(y_{2})+(1-p_{2}')u(y_{1})}{\delta}\Rightarrow u(y_{2})-u(y_{2}-\overline{\nu})<\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{2}')(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 must hold
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
U_{1}<\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{1})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}\\
U_{2}<\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{2}')(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{2}')(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}<\frac{(\frac{1}{N}-p_{1})(u(y_{2})-u(y_{1}))}{\delta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{2}<U_{1}$
\end_inset

 ,
 this equilibrium rests upon the incentive for the poor participant to participate.
 So long as the poorer participant finds the competition worthwhile,
 the rich would also participate.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above results outline how participation costs influence the investments towards status.
 For very low 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

,
 rich and poor would both participate and for very high 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\nu}$
\end_inset

,
 both would withdraw.
 For everything in between,
 the only equilibrium with a diminishing utility from wealth is one where rich participate whereas poor don't.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above framework also allows us to discuss the implications of inequality for the same total wealth in the economy.
 As such,
 a segregation where the poor never participate in the status economy seems less favourable than one where both rich and poor participate in the status competitions.
 The current chapter explains that the likely outcome of a failure to maintain universal participation in status games (under diminishing utility from wealth) is that of a segregation where only the rich participate in skills leading to economic success.
 While a reference-based utility (which may be encouraged with segregation) does seem to permit an equilibrium where only the poor are concerned with status games (with respect to skills-enhancement),
 the result only suggests that a segregation of status games along with that of incomes is another possible long-term outcome of limited participation.
\end_layout

