\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper considers a model where status amounts to a position maintained by the participant's social capital investments.
 It uses a decision theory framework for uncertainty inherent in status considerations and investigates the role of income gaps on expenditures that carry some status while also having an effect on the income mobility of the participants.
 The endogenisation of status in this manner allows an understanding of the role of income gaps in a generic framework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above model provides three important details pertaining to status-expenditures in an environment of income inequalities.
 First,
 there are only two long-term states of the poor-rich relationship that exist for status competitions under a diminishing wealth utility - one where both rich and poor participate in status competitions and another where only rich stand to benefit from status competitions.
 The particular detail demonstrates how income differences limit the benefit from status competitions to the poor - providing a quantitative basis for observations on social limits to growth in the literature 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{HirschSocialLimits,GalbraithBeyondContentment1994,GalbraithAffluent}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 More specifically,
 while poor may be incentivised with status in a setting where status is exclusive to the rich,
 the other long-term equilibrium is one where both rich and poor must strive for status.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second detail highlighted by the model is the interplay of participation costs (threshold) and income-differences in status competitions.
 The model explains how a withdrawal from expenditures such as education could be the result of a combined effect of both participation costs and income-differences.
 More specifically,
 high participation costs push the equilibrium to one where only rich participate in status-related competitions while high income differences may continue to incentivise the poor to participate.
 In other words,
 reducing income-differences without lowering of participation costs in status competitions may have an effect of supporting status-games under long-established inequalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The third detail is the role of a reference-based utility - which serves as a caveat to the effects of income-differences on status expenditures.
 The model shows that contentment at lower levels of wealth could support segregation of competitions at different income levels in the economy.
 In other words,
 status competitions could be feasible despite vast income differences so long as the utility depends on local levels of wealth.
 This also seems in line with many of the findings from subjective contentment data on belief in meritocracy amdist inequalities 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{MijsMeritocracy2020}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

